{{/*
family: set-cli
what: poll
transports: v2c, v3
reference: common set-style SNMP statements (set snmp community …, set snmp v3 …); RFC 3414 (USM) and RFC 3415 (VACM)
verification: reference
*/}}
{{define "does"}}Lets Woodshed poll the device's SNMP agent (SNMP{{.Transport}}, read-only{{if eq .Transport "v2c"}}, from Woodshed's address only{{end}}).{{end}}

{{define "parts"}}
@@part cli - Enter in configuration mode, then commit
{{- if eq .Transport "v2c"}}
set snmp community <COMMUNITY> authorization read-only
set snmp community <COMMUNITY> clients {{.Address}}/{{if .IsIPv6}}128{{else}}32{{end}}
{{- else}}
set snmp v3 usm local-engine user {{.V3User}} {{.Auth.Set}} authentication-password <AUTH-PASSPHRASE>
set snmp v3 usm local-engine user {{.V3User}} {{.Priv.Set}} privacy-password <PRIV-PASSPHRASE>
set snmp view woodshed-all oid .1 include
set snmp v3 vacm security-to-group security-model usm security-name {{.V3User}} group woodshed
set snmp v3 vacm access group woodshed default-context-prefix security-model usm security-level privacy read-view woodshed-all
{{- end}}
commit
{{end}}

{{define "notes"}}
{{if eq .Transport "v2c"}}{{if .IsName}}A client list usually takes addresses, not names: use the address Woodshed polls from.
{{end}}{{else}}The statements follow the SNMPv3 access model (RFC 3415): the user joins a group, and the group may read a view. The keywords for each piece differ between devices.
Add the same user in Woodshed: Settings → SNMP → v3 users, name {{.V3User}}, {{.Auth.Label}} and {{.Priv.Label}}, with the same passphrases.
{{end}}
Then add the device in Woodshed: Devices → Add polling, its address, SNMP{{.Transport}}{{if eq .Transport "v2c"}} and the same community{{else}} and the user {{.V3User}}{{end}}.
{{end}}

{{define "check"}}Devices → Add polling → Test poll answers with the device's name and interfaces; after the first cycle the device page shows its graphs.{{end}}
